/* Generated search index for the user guide. */
window.RTLY_DOCS = [{"t":"Quick start","u":"guides/quick-start.html","d":"Install RTLY, open a supported AI chat, see a Persian answer line up right to left, and learn the three controls you will use most. About five minutes.","h":["1. Install and pin","2. Open a supported site","3. Check the popup","4. Read and write Persian","5. Choose a mode and a font size","6. Switch a site off","What to try next"],"x":"What you will do: install the extension, open a supported site, check the popup, try a mode and a font size, and switch a site off and on. Before you start: Chrome 111 or newer (or Edge with the same package). You need an account on at least one supported site if the site asks for one. You do not need to change any setting. 1. Install and pin # Add RTLY from the Chrome Web Store (other ways to install are in Installation ). Then click the puzzle icon in the toolbar and pin RTLY, so its icon is always visible. 2. Open a supported site # Go to a site such as claude.ai , chatgpt.com or gemini.google.com . RTLY is switched on for every supported site by default. If the tab was already open when you installed RTLY, reload it once. 3. Check the popup # Click the RTLY icon in the toolbar. The popup on a supported site. You should see: the site name and address under \"Current site\", the words \"RTLY active\", a switch that is on, three mode buttons (Full, Font, RTL), a font-size control showing 100%, and a list of all 16 supported sites below. On any other page, the popup says that RTLY does not support the site. That is expected. 4. Read and write Persian # Send a message in Persian, or open a conversation that has Persian answers. Each paragraph is checked on its own. You should see: Persian paragraphs aligned to the right, in the IranYekan font. English paragraphs stay on the left, in the site's own font. Code blocks stay left-to-right. When you type in the message box, each line follows the language you start it in. How RTLY decides is explained in Modes and direction . 5. Choose a mode and a font size # In the popup, click Font . The direction no longer changes, but the Persian font stays. Click RTL for the opposite: direction only, no font. Click Full to get both again. Click A+ or A&minus; to change the text size in steps of 5%, between 85% and 120%. Both changes apply to the open tabs straight away. You do not need to reload. 6. Switch a site off # Turn off the switch in the popup. RTLY reloads the open tabs of that site and then leaves it alone. Turn the switch on to bring RTLY back. Be careful Switching a site on or off reloads its open tabs. If you were in the middle of typing a message, copy it first. What to try next # Open the settings page for the live preview, the interface language and the sites table. Use the shortcut Alt + Shift + R to turn RTLY on or off for the site you are on. If a site looks wrong, read Troubleshooting and then report it . Next Installation"},{"t":"Installation","u":"guides/installation.html","d":"Install RTLY from the Chrome Web Store, in Edge, or as an experimental Firefox build; load it unpacked; update and remove it.","h":["Chrome","Microsoft Edge","Firefox (experimental)","Load it unpacked (Chrome or Edge)","Pin the icon","Update","Remove"],"x":"Chrome # Open the RTLY page on the Chrome Web Store . Click Add to Chrome and confirm. Click the puzzle icon in the toolbar and pin RTLY. RTLY needs Chrome 111 or newer. Chrome updates it automatically. Install prompt Chrome tells you that RTLY can read and change your data on the websites it supports. That access is how RTLY sets direction and font on those pages. It does not apply to any other site. Privacy, permissions and data lists every permission and what it is for. Microsoft Edge # Edge uses the same package as Chrome. You can open the Chrome Web Store page above in Edge, or load the Chrome zip yourself as described in Load it unpacked (use edge://extensions ). The guide author has not tested the Chrome Web Store route in Edge for this release. Firefox (experimental) # RTLY is not on Firefox Add-ons. A Firefox build is published as a zip file on the Releases page. It needs Firefox 128 or newer on a computer. Download rtly-<version>-firefox.zip from the Releases page . In Firefox, open about:debugging#/runtime/this-firefox . Click Load Temporary Add-on and choose the zip file. Firefox removes temporary add-ons when it closes, so you repeat these steps after each restart. Experimental The Firefox package passes Mozilla's web-ext lint check, but it has not been run in a real Firefox by the project's tests. Direction handling, the popup, the shortcut and the right-click menu may behave differently there. Please report what you find . Load it unpacked (Chrome or Edge) # Use this to try a version that is not in the store yet, or to run the source code. Download rtly-<version>-chrome.zip from the Releases page and unzip it. Or, from the source repository, use the extension folder. Open chrome://extensions (or edge://extensions ) and turn on Developer mode . Click Load unpacked and choose the folder that contains manifest.json . An unpacked extension does not update by itself. To update it, replace the folder's files and click the reload button on the extension card. Pin the icon # Click the puzzle icon in the toolbar and click the pin next to RTLY. In Firefox, use the extensions button in the toolbar. Update # Store installs update by themselves. To check by hand in Chrome, open chrome://extensions , turn on Developer mode and click Update . Your settings are kept. Remove # Right-click the RTLY icon and choose Remove from Chrome (or Edge). The settings RTLY saved in your browser are removed with the extension. Nothing is stored anywhere else. Previous Quick start Next The popup"},{"t":"The popup","u":"guides/popup.html","d":"Every control in the RTLY toolbar popup: the current site card, modes, font size, the list of sites, the menu, About and the support reminder.","h":["The current site card","All sites","The menu","The footer","The support reminder","Language and direction"],"x":"Click the RTLY icon in the toolbar to open it. Everything you change here is saved at once. The popup on a supported site. The current site card # When the tab you are on is a supported site , the top of the popup shows a card for it: Control What it does Site name and address Shows which site RTLY recognized, for example Claude and claude.ai . Status line \"RTLY active\" when the site is on, \"RTLY disabled\" when it is off. Switch Turns RTLY on or off for this site. RTLY reloads the site's open tabs after you change it. Full / Font / RTL The mode for this site. A short line under the buttons explains the selected mode. Mode changes apply to open tabs at once. Font size, A&minus; and A+ Changes the text size by 5% per click, from 85% to 120%. This setting is shared by all sites. On any other page, the card says that RTLY does not support the site and asks you to open one of the supported chats. All sites # Below the card is the list of all 16 supported sites with a switch for each, and a count such as \"16 of 16 enabled\". Pinned sites come first and show a star. A site that is in a single-feature mode shows a small \"Font only\" or \"RTL only\" tag, and the site you are on is tagged \"Current\". You choose the pinned sites on the settings page . Until you change it, the pinned sites are ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini and Perplexity. The menu # The three-dot button opens a menu: The popup menu. Settings opens the settings page in a new tab. About RTLY shows the version, the author and a support button. Support RTLY opens the Buy me a coffee page. Theme : light, dark or system. Color palette : teal, blue, indigo or neutral. The heart button in the header does the same as Support RTLY . These links open only when you click them. The footer # The bottom of the popup rotates every few seconds through four short messages: about, source code on GitHub, rate the extension, and support. The GitHub, rating and support messages have a button that opens a page in a new tab. The support reminder # About two weeks after installation, and then at most once a month, RTLY asks once if you want to support the project. Two things happen: While a supported site is in front, a small red heart appears on the toolbar icon. When you open the popup, a card appears with a coffee button, a rating button and \"Not now\". Showing the card clears the heart and starts the next month. The reminder never appears inside a web page. It uses no timer, no alarm and no network request. Nothing in the interface turns it off for good, but it can appear only about once a month. Language and direction # The popup follows the interface language you picked on the settings page, or your browser's language if it is one of the 15 supported languages. Right-to-left languages flip the whole popup. See Languages and appearance . The same popup in English, dark theme. Previous Installation Next The settings page"},{"t":"The settings page","u":"guides/settings.html","d":"The RTLY settings page: live preview, font size, interface language, color palette, theme and the sites table with modes, pins and switches.","h":["Header","Live preview","Display","Sites","Narrow windows"],"x":"Open it from the popup menu ( Settings ), or from your browser's extensions page (the extension's Details and Extension options ). It opens in its own tab. Changes are saved as you make them, and a \"Saved\" note flashes at the top. The settings page. Header # At the top are the RTLY name, a heart button (support) and the theme switch: light, dark or system. The theme is shared with the popup. Live preview # Two panes show the same short chat, \"Without RTLY\" and \"With RTLY\". Tabs above them switch the sample language. The preview follows the font size slider as you drag it, and it follows the interface language you choose. The preview is a picture of the effect, not a live page: it does not read any site. Display # Setting Choices Notes Font size 85% to 120% in steps of 5. Default 100%. Scales chat text on all sites. The popup's A+ and A&minus; buttons change the same value. UI language 15 languages, shown by their own names. Changes the popup and this page. It does not change what the chat sites show. Right-to-left languages flip the layout. Color palette Teal (default), blue, indigo, neutral. Changes the accent color of the popup and this page. Sites # A table lists all 16 supported sites with a search box above it. For each site you can: Column What it does Site The site name and its address, with its logo. Mode Three buttons: Full, Font, RTL. See Modes and direction . Applies to open tabs at once. Pin A star. Pinned sites are listed first in the popup. Without any choice from you, ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini and Perplexity are pinned. Status A switch. Off means RTLY does nothing on that site. RTLY reloads the site's open tabs when you change it. The sites table. Narrow windows # The page adapts to a narrow window, for example when you resize the tab or split the screen. The settings page in a narrow window. Previous The popup Next Modes and direction"},{"t":"Modes and direction","u":"guides/modes-and-direction.html","d":"What the Full, Font and RTL modes do, exactly how RTLY decides a block is right-to-left, what it leaves alone, and what happens when you type or copy.","h":["The three modes","How direction is chosen","The font","The message box","What RTLY leaves alone","Copying text","Font size","Streaming answers"],"x":"The three modes # Mode Direction and alignment IranYekan font Use it when Full (default) Yes Yes You want both. Font No change Yes The site already handles direction, and you only want the font. RTL Yes No You like the site's own font and only want the direction fixed. The mode is set per site, in the popup or on the settings page. A change applies to open tabs at once, without a reload. Switching a site off is separate: that stops RTLY on the site and reloads its tabs. How direction is chosen # For each block of text (a paragraph, a list item, a heading), RTLY counts letters: Letters from right-to-left scripts count as right-to-left: Arabic (which includes Persian, Urdu and others), Hebrew, Syriac, Thaana, N'Ko, Samaritan, Mandaic and Adlam. The English letters A to Z and a to z count as left-to-right. Digits, punctuation, spaces, emoji and letters of other scripts (Cyrillic, Chinese and so on) are not counted at all. If at least 30% of the counted letters are right-to-left, the block becomes right-to-left. The bar is 20% when the block contains a mark that only Arabic-script text uses: the Arabic comma, semicolon or question mark, a vowel mark, or the zero-width non-joiner used in Persian. Otherwise the block stays left-to-right. A block with no counted letters gets no direction. The low bar is on purpose. Persian text is full of English terms such as React or npm , and it should still read right to left. Example Result Why Run npm install را اجرا کن Right-to-left 8 of the 21 counted letters (about 38%) are Persian. Please translate the word سلام for me Left-to-right 4 of 31 counted letters (about 13%) are Persian. A paragraph in English Left-to-right No right-to-left letters. 12345 + 678 No change No letters are counted. When a block is right-to-left, RTLY sets dir , direction and the alignment so the text sits on the right. Left-to-right blocks are set the same way to the left, which looks the same as the site's default. Persian lines inside an English-majority block are still ordered correctly, because each line works out its own base direction. The font # The IranYekan font is bundled in the extension. RTLY applies it only to elements whose text contains Arabic-script characters. If you delete the Persian text from such an element and type English, RTLY takes the font off again, so English text keeps the site's own font and its line height. The message box # The box where you type is not given a fixed direction. RTLY sets it to automatic and the browser follows the first strong letter of each line. Start a line in Persian and it is right-to-left. Start the next line in English and that line is left-to-right. In Font mode RTLY does not touch the direction of the box at all. What RTLY leaves alone # Code. Code blocks and inline code stay left-to-right and are not processed. Sidebars, menus, headers and toolbars. RTLY recognizes them by their tags, roles and names. Conversation titles in the sidebar of Claude and Perplexity are the exception: a title that is mostly Persian is aligned to the right. Icons and buttons that contain icons. Flipping them would swap the icon and its label. Very long containers. A single element with more than 5000 characters of text gets the font but no direction. The paragraphs inside it are handled one by one. The page layout. On sites where Persian text could flip the whole page, RTLY keeps the top-level layout left-to-right so the sidebar stays on its side. Other frames. RTLY works in the top page only, not in embedded frames. Copying text # When you copy a selection that is mostly Persian from a right-to-left block, RTLY puts an invisible right-to-left mark in front of the text on your clipboard. This helps the text keep its direction where you paste it. Selections that are mostly English, and anything inside code, are copied as they are. For a Persian selection only plain text is copied, so formatting is not included. Font size # The font size setting scales the page's base font size by a pe"},{"t":"Shortcut and right-click menu","u":"guides/shortcut-and-menu.html","d":"Turn RTLY on or off for the current site with Alt+Shift+R or the right-click menu, and change the shortcut in your browser.","h":["Keyboard shortcut","Right-click menu","What happens"],"x":"Keyboard shortcut # Press Alt + Shift + R on a supported site. RTLY flips the setting for that site and reloads its open tabs. The shortcut does nothing on a page that is not one of the supported sites . The key is a suggestion. Your browser keeps another extension's or its own shortcut if it clashes. You can see and change the key here: Chrome: chrome://extensions/shortcuts Edge: edge://extensions/shortcuts Firefox: about:addons , then the gear button and Manage Extension Shortcuts Right-click menu # Right-click anywhere on the page, or right-click the RTLY toolbar icon, and choose the RTLY item (in English: \"RTLY: Enable/disable for this site\"). It does the same as the shortcut. The item is also translated into the browser languages that RTLY has a translation for. What happens # RTLY finds which supported site the tab belongs to. If the site was on (or never set), it turns it off. If it was off, it turns it on. RTLY reloads every open tab of that site, so the change takes effect everywhere. Be careful The reload loses anything you have not sent. If you were writing a message, copy it first. There is no message on screen to confirm the change. Open the popup, or look at the page, to see the result. Previous Modes and direction Next Supported sites"},{"t":"Supported sites","u":"guides/supported-sites.html","d":"The 16 AI chat sites RTLY works on, their addresses, and what to do if your site is not on the list.","h":["How well each site works","Sites that are not supported","Ask for another site"],"x":"Site Address Notes ChatGPT chatgpt.com The older address chat.openai.com is also covered. Claude claude.ai Gemini gemini.google.com Perplexity perplexity.ai Microsoft Copilot copilot.microsoft.com Google AI Studio aistudio.google.com NotebookLM notebooklm.google.com Only the chat section is processed. Grok grok.com Poe poe.com Z.ai z.ai , chat.z.ai DeepSeek chat.deepseek.com Qwen qwen.ai , chat.qwen.ai Bing bing.com RTLY runs on all of bing.com, not only the chat. Mistral mistral.ai , chat.mistral.ai RTLY runs on the whole host. Hugging Face huggingface.co/chat Only the /chat section. Cohere cohere.com , coral.cohere.com RTLY runs on the whole host. How well each site works # Each site has its own small set of rules, because each site builds its pages differently. The project tests five of them in a real Chromium browser, with local copies of their page structure: ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, Perplexity and Mistral. The other eleven are covered by consistency checks only. None of the tests loads the live sites. Sites change AI sites change their page markup without notice. When that happens RTLY can stop working on a site, or work only partly, until its rules are updated. If a site looks wrong, send a broken-site report with the address and a screenshot. Sites that are not supported # Any site that is not in the table: other chat tools, search engines other than Bing's chat pages, social networks and so on. RTLY does not run there, and it has no permission to. The address matters. For example, x.com (Grok inside X) and Google's AI Overviews in search results are not covered. Hugging Face is covered only under /chat . Ask for another site # Open a feature request with the site address. Adding a site means writing its rules and giving the extension permission for its address, so some requests will be declined to keep the permission list short. If you write code, the contributing guide in the repository explains how to add a site. Previous Shortcut and right-click menu Next Languages and appearance"},{"t":"Languages and appearance","u":"guides/languages-and-appearance.html","d":"The 15 interface languages of RTLY, how the language is chosen, what is still English, and the light and dark themes and color palettes.","h":["Interface languages","How the language is chosen","Text the browser shows","The chat itself","Themes","Color palettes","This guide"],"x":"The interface language list on the settings page. Interface languages # Fourteen of the 15 languages are written right to left, and the whole popup and settings page flip with them. Language Name shown in the picker Author's rating Strings still in English English English Source language 0 Persian فارسی Marked as reviewed 0 Arabic العربية Marked as reviewed 0 Hebrew עברית Marked as reviewed 0 Urdu اردو High confidence 0 Pashto پښتو Good 8 Central Kurdish کوردیی ناوەندی Good 8 Sindhi سنڌي Lowest confidence 8 Uyghur ئۇيغۇرچە Good 8 Yiddish ייִדיש High confidence 8 Western Punjabi پنجابی (شاہمُکھی) Strongest of the five newer ones 8 South Azerbaijani تۆرکجه Draft, needs native review 8 Saraiki سرائیکی Draft, needs native review 8 Balochi بلوچی Draft, needs native review 8 Kashmiri کٲشُر Draft, needs native review 8 The rating column is the author's own assessment, taken from the source. No translation was checked by an independent native speaker for this guide. If you can improve one, use the translation fix form or send a pull request. How the language is chosen # The language you pick in UI language on the settings page , if you have picked one. Otherwise, your browser's language, if it is one of the 15 (a region such as fa-IR is reduced to fa ). Otherwise, English. A missing translation falls back to English, word by word. Text the browser shows # A few strings are not part of the page: the extension's name and description on the extensions page, the shortcut's label and the right-click menu item. These come from the browser's own translation system. They exist in English, Persian, Arabic, Hebrew, Urdu, Pashto, Central Kurdish, Sindhi, Uyghur and Yiddish. For Western Punjabi, South Azerbaijani, Saraiki, Balochi and Kashmiri the browser shows English there. The chat itself # The interface language has nothing to do with the language of your chats. RTLY decides each message's direction from its own text, in any language, whatever the interface language is. Themes # Choose light , dark or system from the popup menu or the settings header. With system , RTLY follows your operating system. The theme applies to RTLY's own pages; it does not change the chat sites. Color palettes # Teal is the default. Blue, indigo and neutral (gray) change only the accent color. Pick one in the popup menu or in Color palette on the settings page. This guide # This guide has a light and a dark theme too (the button in the header). Its home page, installation page, quick start and privacy page are also available in Persian . Previous Supported sites Next Privacy, permissions and data"},{"t":"Privacy, permissions and data","u":"guides/privacy-and-data.html","d":"Which permissions RTLY asks for and why, what it stores on your device, what it reads on a page, and what leaves your browser (nothing).","h":["The short version","Permissions","What is saved on your device","What the website sees","Links","This website","Full policy"],"x":"The short version # RTLY makes no network requests of its own. There is nothing to send data to. It reads the text of messages on the supported sites, in your browser, to decide their direction. It does not store that text and does not send it anywhere. It saves your settings in your browser's extension storage, on your device. It contains no analytics, advertising, tracking or remote code. Permissions # Permission Why RTLY needs it Access to the supported sites (27 address patterns, all https ) To run on those pages, read their text to choose a direction, and set direction, alignment and font. The install prompt words this as reading and changing your data on those sites. RTLY has no access to other sites. storage To save your settings on your device. contextMenus To add the right-click item that turns RTLY on or off for a site. RTLY does not ask for the tabs, history, cookies, downloads, notifications or alarms permissions, and it cannot read your other tabs or your browsing history. It makes the extension's two font files available to the supported sites, so the stylesheet it adds can load them. That also means those sites could detect that RTLY is installed. What is saved on your device # Setting Meaning Site switches Which sites you turned off. A site not in the list is on. Site modes Full, Font or RTL for each site you changed. Pinned sites Which sites you pinned in the popup. Font size A number from 85 to 120. Theme and color palette Your choice for RTLY's own pages. Interface language The language you picked. Support reminder When RTLY was installed and when it last showed the reminder, so it can wait a month. That is all. Your chats, prompts and answers are never saved by RTLY. Removing the extension removes these settings. What the website sees # The site you are on can see its own page after RTLY changed it: RTLY adds attributes such as dir and data-rtly-* , sets styles on text elements, and adds a stylesheet and a font. RTLY does not hide this. It does not read what you type to send it anywhere, and it does not change what the site sends. Links # The popup and settings page contain links to the project's GitHub page, to the Buy me a coffee page and to the store's reviews page. They open in a new tab only when you click them. Those sites have their own privacy practices. This website # This guide is hosted on GitHub Pages. GitHub may record visits to it, as any web host does. The extension never contacts it. The guide loads no scripts or fonts from other sites and uses your browser's local storage only to remember the light or dark theme you chose. Full policy # The full text is the privacy policy . Previous Languages and appearance Next Troubleshooting and FAQ"},{"t":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","u":"guides/troubleshooting-faq.html","d":"Answers to common RTLY problems: nothing changes, the font is missing, English is flipped, the shortcut does nothing, a reload happened, and how to report a broken site.","h":["Nothing changes on a site","The direction is right but the font is not","An English paragraph is aligned to the right, or a Persian one to the left","A code block or table looks wrong","I type Persian but the line does not go right","The shortcut does nothing","The page reloaded when I switched RTLY","A red heart appeared on the toolbar icon","Some words in the RTLY popup are in English","Firefox: RTLY disappeared after a restart","How to report a problem","Questions"],"x":"Nothing changes on a site # Check the address is one of the supported sites . Open the popup: it should show the site name and \"RTLY active\". If the switch is off, turn it on. RTLY reloads the tab. If you installed RTLY while the tab was open, reload the tab once. Check the mode. Font keeps the direction as the site sets it. RTL keeps the site's font. Make sure there is Persian (or other right-to-left) text in the block. A block with no such letters is left as it is. If everything is right and it still fails, the site has probably changed its page. Report it . The direction is right but the font is not # The font is applied only to text that contains Arabic-script characters, and not in RTL mode. In Full or Font mode, an element that RTLY skipped (for example one that contains an icon) may keep the site's font. An English paragraph is aligned to the right, or a Persian one to the left # Direction comes from the share of right-to-left letters in a block: at least 30% (or 20% with an Arabic-script punctuation or vowel mark). A Persian sentence made mostly of English terms can fall below that bar, and an English paragraph that quotes a lot of Persian can rise above it. See the examples in Modes and direction . If a normal paragraph is wrong, report it. A code block or table looks wrong # RTLY leaves code blocks left-to-right and does not process them. If the surrounding text or a table is misaligned, it is probably a site-specific rule. Report it with a screenshot. I type Persian but the line does not go right # The message box follows the first strong letter of each line. A line that begins with an English word or a number is left-to-right. Begin the line with a Persian word, or press Enter and start a new line. In Font mode RTLY does not touch the box's direction. The shortcut does nothing # The shortcut works on supported sites only. Your browser may have given Alt + Shift + R to something else. Check the shortcuts page (see Shortcut and right-click menu ) and set a free key. The page reloaded when I switched RTLY # That is by design when you switch a site on or off. Changing the mode or the font size does not reload. A red heart appeared on the toolbar icon # That is the monthly support reminder . Open the popup to see it and to clear the heart. Some words in the RTLY popup are in English # In ten of the interface languages eight strings are still English. See Languages and appearance . Firefox: RTLY disappeared after a restart # The Firefox build is loaded as a temporary add-on, which Firefox removes when it closes. Load it again from about:debugging . See Installation . How to report a problem # Open a broken-site report (for a site that looks wrong) or a bug report (for RTLY itself). Include: the page address (use a chat with no private content), a screenshot, with private text hidden, your browser and the RTLY version (shown in the popup header and on the extensions page), whether RTLY is on for that site in the popup. Security problems must not go in a public issue. Use private reporting . Questions # Does RTLY read my conversations? It reads the text of messages on the page, inside your browser, to choose a direction. It never stores or sends it. Does it work offline? Yes. It needs no server. The chat sites themselves need a connection. Does it work in private (incognito) windows? Only if you allow it for private windows in the browser's extension settings. This was not tested for this guide. Does it slow the page down? RTLY is written to do little work: it skips unchanged text, avoids layout reads and works in short slices. It has been checked by hand on long ChatGPT conversations, not by an automated performance test. Can I use it on mobile? RTLY is built for desktop browsers. Mobile browsers were not tested. Why is it free? It is a hobby project, released under the MIT license. If it saves you time, you can buy the author a coffee . Previous Privacy, permissions and data Next Limits and known problems"},{"t":"Limits and known problems","u":"guides/limits.html","d":"An honest list of what RTLY cannot do, what has not been tested, and what is known to be incomplete.","h":["What RTLY does not do","Behavior limits","What has not been verified","Known oddities","Found something else?"],"x":"What RTLY does not do # It does not translate, spell-check or rewrite anything. It does not change the language of the sites' own interface. It does not work on sites outside the list of 16 . It does not process embedded frames, only the top page. It does not keep its settings in sync between browsers or computers. Behavior limits # Direction is decided from letters only. Digits, punctuation and letters of other scripts (for example Cyrillic or Chinese) are not counted. A single container with more than 5000 characters of text gets the font but no direction. Blocks that contain an icon get the font but no direction on most sites. Code is always left-to-right. The font size changes the page's base size. Text sized in fixed pixels does not scale. Switching a site on or off reloads its open tabs. The Bing, Mistral and Cohere entries cover their whole host, so RTLY also runs on pages there that are not chat pages. For Bing this includes search results. What has not been verified # Be aware These are limits of the testing, not known failures. Live sites. The automated tests use local fixture pages that imitate the structure of five sites (ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, Perplexity, Mistral). They do not load the real sites, and the other eleven sites have no browser test. Sites change without notice, so a passing test does not prove a site works today. Firefox. The Firefox package passes Mozilla's lint, but it has not been run in a real Firefox by the project's tests. It is experimental. Edge and other Chromium browsers. They use the Chrome package. They were not tested separately. Translations. Not reviewed by native speakers apart from the author's own. In ten languages eight strings are still English, and the browser-level text (name, description, shortcut label) is translated for 10 of the 15 languages. Accessibility. The popup and settings page use labeled controls and keyboard-operable switches. They were not tested with a screen reader. Mobile browsers and private windows. Not tested. Known oddities # The font file is made available to the supported sites, so a site could detect that RTLY is installed. A few site-specific style rules are loaded on those sites even when you switch RTLY off for them. They are limited to small layout variables; whether any of them has a visible effect on a disabled site has not been tested. The About text inside the extension still reads \"All rights reserved\", while the project is MIT licensed. The license in the repository is the one that applies. Found something else? # Please open an issue . See Troubleshooting for what to include. Previous Troubleshooting and FAQ"},{"t":"Privacy policy","u":"privacy.html","d":"The RTLY privacy policy: what the extension accesses, what it stores on your device, and what leaves your browser.","h":["Summary","What RTLY can access","What RTLY stores on your device","What leaves your browser","Permissions","The websites you use","Third parties","Children","Changes to this policy","Contact"],"x":"Last updated: 2026-10-08. This policy applies to RTLY 2.0.0 and later versions until it is updated. Summary # RTLY runs inside your browser. It has no server, no account and no analytics. It does not collect, store or send the content of your chats. It saves a few settings on your device. What RTLY can access # Pages of the supported AI chat sites. RTLY runs only on the sites listed in the user guide (27 address patterns for 16 sites). On those pages it reads the text to decide whether each block is right-to-left, and it changes the page's text direction, alignment and font. The text is read in memory. RTLY does not save it, copy it, log it or send it anywhere. The address of the active tab, on those sites only. The extension uses it to know which site the shortcut or the right-click menu refers to, and whether to show the support heart on the toolbar icon. RTLY cannot access other websites, your bookmarks, history, cookies, downloads or files, or the content of your other tabs. What RTLY stores on your device # RTLY keeps these settings in your browser's extension storage ( chrome.storage.local ). RTLY does not use browser sync for them. Setting Meaning Site switches Which sites you turned off Site modes Full, Font or RTL for each site you changed Pinned sites The sites you pinned in the popup Font size A number from 85 to 120 Theme and color palette Your choice for RTLY's own pages Interface language The language you picked Support reminder The time of installation and the time the reminder was last shown, so it can wait about a month Your chats, prompts and answers are never stored by RTLY. The settings stay until you remove the extension. Removing the extension removes them. What leaves your browser # Nothing. RTLY makes no network requests. There is no server to receive data, and the extension contains no analytics, advertising, tracking or remote code. The popup and settings page have links to the project's GitHub page, to the Buy me a coffee page and to the store's reviews page. They open in a new tab only when you click them. Once opened, those sites receive your request from your browser and follow their own privacy policies. Permissions # Permission Why Access to the supported sites ( https only) To run on those pages, read their text to choose a direction, and set direction, alignment and font storage To save the settings above contextMenus To add the right-click item that turns RTLY on or off for a site RTLY does not request the tabs, history, cookies, downloads, notifications or alarms permissions. The websites you use # An AI chat site can see its own page after RTLY has changed it, for example the dir and data-rtly-* attributes and the styles RTLY adds. RTLY makes its two font files available to the supported sites so that its stylesheet can load them. A site could use this to detect that RTLY is installed. RTLY does not change what a site sends or receives. Third parties # Browser stores (Chrome Web Store, Microsoft Edge Add-ons, Firefox Add-ons) keep their own install statistics. RTLY does not see them. GitHub hosts the source code, the releases and the user guide. GitHub may record visits to those pages, as any web host does. The extension itself never contacts GitHub. Buy me a coffee is contacted only if you click a support link. Children # RTLY collects no personal information from anyone, including children. Changes to this policy # If RTLY changes what it accesses or stores, this page will be updated with a new date, and the change will be listed in the changelog. Contact # Ask a question or report a problem in an issue . Report a security problem privately through GitHub private vulnerability reporting . RTLY is written and maintained by Ehsan Enaloo."}];
